  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nMid level high centered over the Ozarks will slowly translate east \ninto the Tennessee Valley through Wednesday. Temperatures will \ncontinue to progressively get warmer with each passing day during \nthis time frame. Suspect that guidance is a bit bullish. While soils \nare drying out, there are still lingering wet conditions across the \nnorthern half of the area. Nonetheless, after today heat indices will\nbe around 100.\n\nOn Thursday, the mid level ridge starts to get suppressed slightly\nsouthwards. A front that lays out across the lower Great Lakes may be\nin close enough proximity to bring at least a low chance of storms\nThursday afternoon and evening in the north. The affect on\ntemperatures looks minimal. And in the south, this may end up being\nthe hottest day.\n\nA mid level trough over eastern Canada will dig as it moves southeast\ntowards the end of the week. This will cause the ridge to retrograde\nback into the Plains. The front that was across the Great Lakes on \nThursday will push into, and most likely through, the area. The\ngreatest chance of storms will be on Friday. These changes will also\nresult in temperatures not being as high, although still remaining\nabove normal.",
